!!Hatra!!
([[ /ˈhætrə/ | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEnglish ]]; Arabic:
الحَضْر (Arabic pronunciation:
[[ [al.ˈħadˤr\] | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FArabic ]]);
Syriac: ܚܛܪܐ) was an ancient city in [[ Upper Mesopotamia ]] located in
present-day eastern [[ Nineveh Governorate ]] in northern [[ Iraq ]]. The
ruins of the city lie 290 km (180 mi) northwest of [[ Baghdad ]] and 110 km
(68 mi) southwest of [[ Mosul ]]. It is considered the richest archaeological
site from the [[ Parthian Empire ]] known to date.[[ ^1 ]]

Hatra was a strongly fortified [[ caravan city ]] and capital of the small
[[ Kingdom of Hatra ]], located between the [[ Roman | roman-empire ]] and
[[ Parthian | parthian-empire ]]/[[ Sasanian Empires | sasanian-empire ]].
Hatra flourished in the 2nd century, and was [[ destroyed | fall-of-hatra ]]
by the [[ Sassanid Empire ]] and deserted in the 3rd century alongside other
Neo-Assyrian populated states such as [[ Assur ]], [[ Adiabene ]],
[[ Osroene ]] and [[ Beth Nuhadra ]]. Its ruins were discovered in the 19th
century.[[ ^2 ]]

== Name

Hatra is known as //al-Ḥaḍr// (الحضر) in [[ Arabic ]]. It is recorded as
𐣧𐣨𐣣𐣠 (//ḥṭrʾ//, vocalized as: //Ḥaṭrāʾ//) in [[ Hatran Aramaic ]]
inscriptions, probably meaning "enclosure, hedge, fence". In
[[ Syriac | syriac-language ]], it is usually recorded in the plural form
//Ḥaṭrē//. In Roman works, it is recorded as Greek //Átra// and Latin
//Hatra// and //Hatris//.[[ ^2 ]]

The temple of the local [[ Assyrian | assyrian-people ]] god
[[ Shamash ]],[[ ^3 ]] was officially called //Beit ʾElāhāʾ// 𐣡𐣩𐣵 𐣠𐣫𐣤𐣠‎
"House of God", in Mesopotamian Aramaic inscriptions[[ ^4 ]] The city was
recorded as "Enclosure of [[ Shamash ]]" (//ḥtrʾ d-šmš// 𐣧𐣨𐣣𐣠 𐣣𐣴𐣬𐣴‎)
on a coin.[[ ^2 ]]

== History

There is no archeological information on the city before the Parthian period
but settlement in the area likely dates back to at least the
[[ Seleucid | seleucid-empire ]] period.[[ ^2 ]] Hatra flourished under the
[[ Parthians | parthian-empire ]], during the 1st and 2nd centuries CE, as a
religious and trading center.[[ ^5 ]] Later on, the city became the capital of
possibly the first [[ Arab ]]-ruled kingdom in the chain of largely
[[ Arab-controlled cities | pre-islamic-arabia#north-arabia ]] running from
Hatra, in the northeast, via [[ Palmyra ]], [[ Baalbek ]] and [[ Petra ]], in
the southwest. The region controlled from Hatra was the
[[ Kingdom of Hatra ]], a semi-autonomous buffer kingdom on the western limits
of the Parthian Empire, governed by Arabian princes.

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Coin_of_Hatra.jpg/250px-Coin_of_Hatra.jpg" height="125" width="250"

  <figcaption>bronze coin struck in Hatra circa 117–138 AD. Obverse depicts radiate bust of Shamash

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Hatra_plan_general.svg/250px-Hatra_plan_general.svg.png" height="235" width="250"

  <figcaption>Plan of Hatra

Hatra became an important fortified frontier city and played an important role
in the [[ Second Parthian War | romanparthian-wars ]], withstanding repeated
attacks by the [[ Roman Empire ]]. During the 2nd century CE, the city
repulsed sieges by both [[ Trajan ]] (116/117) and
[[ Septimius Severus (198/199). | siege-of-hatra-197198 ]][[ ^6 ]] Hatra's
forces defeated the ascendant [[ Sassanid Persians | sasanian-empire ]] in 238
at the battle of Shahrazoor, but fell shortly after in 241 to the army of
Sassanid king [[ Shapur I ]] and was destroyed.[[ ^6 ]] The traditional
stories of the [[ fall of Hatra ]] tell of [[ al-Nadirah ]], daughter of the
king of Araba, who betrayed the city into the hands of Shapur as she fell in
love with him. The story tells of how Shapur killed the king and married
al-Nadirah, but later had her killed also after realizing her ingratitude
towards her father.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^7 ]]

Hatra was the best preserved and most informative example of ancient Arabian
architecture. Its plan was circular,[[ ^8 ]] and was encircled by inner and
outer walls nearly 2 kilometres (1.2 mi) in diameter[[ ^9 ]] and supported by
more than 160 towers.

=== Religion

A [[ temenos ]] (τέμενος) surrounded the principal sacred buildings in the
city's centre. The temples covered some 1.2 hectares and were dominated by the
Great Temple, an enormous structure with [[ vaults | vault-architecture ]] and
[[ columns | column ]] that once rose to 30 metres. The city was famed for its
fusion of [[ Greek | religion-in-ancient-greece ]],
[[ Assyrian | mesopotamian-religion ]], [[ Canaanite | canaanite-religion ]],
[[ Aramean ]] and Arabian [[ pantheons | pantheon-gods ]], known in
[[ Aramaic | aramaic-language ]] as //Beiṯ Ĕlāhā// ("House of God"). The city
had temples to [[ Nergal ]]
([[ Assyrian | assyria ]]-[[ Babylonian | babylonian-mythology ]] and
[[ Akkadian | akkadian-empire ]]), [[ Hermes ]]
([[ Greek | ancient-greek-religion ]]), [[ Atargatis ]]
([[ Syro-Aramaean | elohim-gods ]]), [[ Allat ]], Shamiyyah
([[ Arabian | arabian-mythology ]]), and [[ Shamash ]] (the
[[ Mesopotamian | mesopotamian-mythology ]] sun god).[[ ^5 ]] Other deities
mentioned in the Hatran Aramaic inscriptions were the Aramaean
[[ Ba'al Shamayn | baal-shamîm ]], and the female deity known as Ashurbel,
which was perhaps the assimilation of the two deities the Assyrian god
[[ Ashur | ashur-god ]] and the Assyrian-Babylonian
[[ Bel | bel-mythology ]]—despite their being individually masculine.

== List of rulers

In inscriptions found at Hatra, several rulers are mentioned. Other rulers are
sporadically mentioned by classical authors. The earlier rulers are titled
//mrjʾ// (//māryā//, "lord") as with the Assyrian rulers of nearby
[[ Assur ]], the later ones //mlkʾ d-ʿrb// ("king of the Arabs"; //malkā//,
"king").[[ ^2 ]]

